<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Char Bouba war</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1673">1673</a>–<a href="/years/1677">1677</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Shurbubba</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Char Bouba war was a conflict in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zawaya">Zawaya</a> scholarly tribes, led by the reforming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> Nasir al-Din, and Hassani Arab warrior groups. The Hassani won by about 1677. The outcome fixed a lasting division of society into warrior and scholarly classes and led to the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a> of Trarza and Brakna.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/char-bouba-war.jpg" alt="Char Bouba war" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anoniem / Anonymous (land surveyor / mapmaker), Anoniem / Anonymous (publisher) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AMH-8160-KB_Map_of_the_regions_between_the_Senegal_and_Gambra_rivers.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
